{
  "id": "m2l3",
  "title": {
    "es": "El bucle for y range",
    "en": "The for loop and range"
  },
  "theory": {
    "es": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Recorrer elementos"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "El bucle `for` recorre los elementos de una secuencia (una lista, un texto, un rango...) uno a uno, sin que tengas que llevar tú el contador."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "for letra in 'abc':",
          "    print(letra)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "a",
          "b",
          "c"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "range: secuencias de números"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "`range(fin)` genera números desde 0 hasta `fin - 1`. `range(inicio, fin)` empieza en `inicio`. `range(inicio, fin, paso)` salta de `paso` en `paso`."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "for i in range(3):",
          "    print(i)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "0",
          "1",
          "2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "for i in range(2, 9, 2):",
          "    print(i)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "2",
          "4",
          "6",
          "8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "for frente a while"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "Usa `for` cuando sabes sobre qué vas a iterar (un rango, una lista). Usa `while` cuando repites hasta que ocurra algo y no sabes cuántas veces será."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Acumular con for"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "El patrón acumulador también funciona con `for`, y suele quedar más limpio."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "total = 0",
          "for n in range(1, 6):",
          "    total = total + n",
          "print(total)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "value": "`len(texto)` te da su longitud, y `for c in texto` recorre sus caracteres. Combinándolos resuelves muchísimos ejercicios de cadenas."
      }
    ],
    "en": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Going through elements"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "The `for` loop goes through the elements of a sequence (a list, a string, a range...) one by one, without you having to keep a counter."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "for letter in 'abc':",
          "    print(letter)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "a",
          "b",
          "c"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "range: number sequences"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "`range(stop)` generates numbers from 0 to `stop - 1`. `range(start, stop)` begins at `start`. `range(start, stop, step)` jumps by `step`."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "for i in range(3):",
          "    print(i)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "0",
          "1",
          "2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "for i in range(2, 9, 2):",
          "    print(i)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "2",
          "4",
          "6",
          "8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "for versus while"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "Use `for` when you know what you will iterate over (a range, a list). Use `while` when you repeat until something happens and don't know how many times."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "value": "Accumulating with for"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "The accumulator pattern also works with `for`, and usually reads cleaner."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "value": [
          "total = 0",
          "for n in range(1, 6):",
          "    total = total + n",
          "print(total)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "output",
        "value": [
          "15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "value": "`len(text)` gives its length, and `for c in text` walks its characters. Combining them solves many string exercises."
      }
    ]
  },
  "exercises": [
    {
      "id": "m2l3e1",
      "prompt": {
        "es": "Define `factorial(n)` que devuelva `n!` = `1 * 2 * ... * n` usando un `for`. Por definición, `factorial(0)` es 1.",
        "en": "Define `factorial(n)` returning `n!` = `1 * 2 * ... * n` using a `for`. By definition `factorial(0)` is 1."
      },
      "starter_code": [
        "def factorial(n):",
        "    resultado = 1",
        "    # usa for y range",
        "    return resultado"
      ],
      "solution": [
        "def factorial(n):",
        "    resultado = 1",
        "    for i in range(2, n + 1):",
        "        resultado = resultado * i",
        "    return resultado"
      ],
      "hints": {
        "es": [
          "Empieza el acumulador en 1 (no en 0, porque multiplicas).",
          "Recorre range(2, n+1) multiplicando."
        ],
        "en": [
          "Start the accumulator at 1 (not 0, since you multiply).",
          "Loop range(2, n+1) multiplying."
        ]
      },
      "tests": [
        {
          "type": "function",
          "name": "factorial",
          "function": "factorial",
          "cases": [
            {
              "args": [
                5
              ],
              "expected": 120
            },
            {
              "args": [
                0
              ],
              "expected": 1
            },
            {
              "args": [
                1
              ],
              "expected": 1
            },
            {
              "args": [
                6
              ],
              "expected": 720
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "m2l3e2",
      "prompt": {
        "es": "Define `cuenta_vocales(texto)` que devuelva cuántas vocales (a, e, i, o, u) hay en `texto`, sin distinguir mayúsculas de minúsculas.",
        "en": "Define `cuenta_vocales(texto)` returning how many vowels (a, e, i, o, u) are in `texto`, ignoring case."
      },
      "starter_code": [
        "def cuenta_vocales(texto):",
        "    contador = 0",
        "    # recorre el texto",
        "    return contador"
      ],
      "solution": [
        "def cuenta_vocales(texto):",
        "    contador = 0",
        "    for c in texto.lower():",
        "        if c in 'aeiou':",
        "            contador = contador + 1",
        "    return contador"
      ],
      "hints": {
        "es": [
          "texto.lower() pasa todo a minúsculas.",
          "if c in 'aeiou' comprueba si la letra es vocal."
        ],
        "en": [
          "texto.lower() lowercases everything.",
          "if c in 'aeiou' checks whether the letter is a vowel."
        ]
      },
      "tests": [
        {
          "type": "function",
          "name": "cuenta_vocales",
          "function": "cuenta_vocales",
          "cases": [
            {
              "args": [
                "hola"
              ],
              "expected": 2
            },
            {
              "args": [
                "MURCIA"
              ],
              "expected": 3
            },
            {
              "args": [
                "xyz"
              ],
              "expected": 0
            },
            {
              "args": [
                ""
              ],
              "expected": 0
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}